1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,190
Imagine sending a research file from Lagos to Cape Town. Watch the route.

2
00:00:06,290 --> 00:00:11,670
It heads north, crosses into Europe, then turns back towards southern Africa.

3
00:00:11,770 --> 00:00:17,300
For years, much of the research traffic between these two African regions took just such a detour.

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:23,290
What would it take to draw a different line on this map?

5
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:26,100
The first pieces were already in place.

6
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:32,900
In 2004, EUMEDCONNECT began linking research communities around the Mediterranean.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,500
In 2005, African network leaders formed the UbuntuNet Alliance

8
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:43,110
to bring more affordable connections to universities in eastern and southern Africa.

9
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:47,300
In 2010, WACREN emerged in West and Central Africa,

10
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:54,200
and the Arab States Research and Education Network, ASREN, brought together partners across the Arab world.

11
00:00:54,300 --> 00:01:00,100
From 2011, the European Union and African partners co-funded AfricaConnect.

12
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:07,710
A university could connect through its national network and reach colleagues across borders.

13
00:01:07,810 --> 00:01:11,500
In Kampala, the difference showed up on a hospital bill.

14
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:17,570
Nsambya Hospital had struggled with expensive bandwidth and an unreliable connection.

15
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:21,900
In 2019, it joined Uganda’s research and education network.

16
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,570
The price it paid for bandwidth fell to one fifth of the previous rate.

17
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:33,300
Network engineers also helped redesign the hospital’s own system at no extra charge,

18
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,680
giving staff a more dependable connection.

19
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:44,920
In Benin and Burkina Faso, the next question was what students would find once their campuses were connected.

20
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:49,700
Through the Africa Digital Campus project, WACREN and its partners equipped lecturers

21
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:54,440
to record lessons and share them on a regional video platform.

22
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:59,500
The project trained a hundred and thirty-four university educators and researchers.

23
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:05,100
Together, they produced two hundred and seventy-seven online courses.

24
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:11,710
A student could go back and replay the part of a lesson that needed a second look.

25
00:02:11,810 --> 00:02:16,500
Around Agadir in Morocco, researchers wanted to see how the land was changing.

26
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:21,690
Each satellite image was about two hundred and fifty megabytes.

27
00:02:21,790 --> 00:02:26,300
Morocco’s academic network and EUMEDCONNECT let the team exchange these files

28
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:30,640
with specialists in Toulouse, who had the software to classify them.

29
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:36,300
Together they mapped changes in argan woodland and traced the city’s roads.

30
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:43,470
The results were used to train Agadir’s municipal transport staff to work with geographic data.

31
00:02:43,570 --> 00:02:47,500
In Oman, a technical college that belongs to the national research network

32
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,100
can use Masader, the country’s virtual science library.

33
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:57,500
Students and lecturers can read scientific journals, electronic books and Arabic publications

34
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:02,240
through subscriptions shared across participating institutions.

35
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:06,300
In Jordan, ASREN’s international connection supports SESAME,

36
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:11,730
a laboratory where researchers use intense beams of light to study materials.

37
00:03:11,830 --> 00:03:18,300
One team used X-rays to look inside ancient glazed beads and fragments of Egyptian blue pigment.

38
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:26,280
The scans revealed clues to how craftspeople made them, while the objects themselves stayed intact.

39
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:28,500
Now send that file again.

40
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:34,300
In 2025, a direct ten-gigabit link connected Lagos and Cape Town.

41
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:38,890
Research traffic between the two regions could stay in Africa.

42
00:03:38,990 --> 00:03:41,900
AfricaConnect Four is extending this work,

43
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,870
while EUMEDplus supports research connectivity in North Africa with partners in Lebanon and Jordan.

44
00:03:48,970 --> 00:03:52,500
On the map, the change is a shorter line.

45
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:56,900
In daily life, it is a hospital paying less, a lecturer sharing a course,

46
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,900
and a student opening a journal her college can now afford.

